  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q21-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "21",
    "prompt": "What is the expected number of tosses of a fair coin required for a head to be obtained:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "once",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2$ tosses.",
          "worked": "Let $E$ be the expected number of tosses. A head finishes the process; a tail uses one toss and restarts it.\n$$\\begin{align*}E&=\\frac12\\times1+\\frac12(1+E)\\\\E&=1+\\frac12E\\\\E&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe expected number is $2$ tosses.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "twice in a row",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$6$ tosses.\n\nUse first-step equations for the starting state and the state immediately after a head.",
          "worked": "Let $E$ be the expected number of tosses from the start, and $F$ the expected additional number immediately after a head. Each toss is equally likely to be heads or tails.\n$$\\begin{align*}E&=\\frac12(F+1)+\\frac12(E+1)\\\\F&=\\frac12\\times1+\\frac12(E+1)\\\\F&=\\frac12E+1.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute for $F$:\n$$\\begin{align*}E&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac12E+2\\right)+\\frac12(E+1)\\\\E&=\\frac34E+\\frac32\\\\E-\\frac34E&=\\frac32\\\\\\frac14E&=\\frac32\\\\E&=6.\\end{align*}$$\nThe expected number is $6$ tosses.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q21-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$n$ times in a row. Warning: This is hard!",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2^{n+1}-2$ tosses.\n\nUse $E_n=2E_{n-1}+2$, with $E_1=2$.",
          "worked": "Let $E_n$ be the expected number of tosses to obtain $n$ consecutive heads. First obtain $n-1$ consecutive heads, taking $E_{n-1}$ tosses on average. The next toss uses one toss: a head finishes, while a tail restarts the process.\n$$\\begin{align*}E_n&=E_{n-1}+1+\\frac12E_n\\\\\\frac12E_n&=E_{n-1}+1\\\\E_n&=2E_{n-1}+2.\\end{align*}$$\nFrom part (a), $E_1=2$. Therefore\n$$\\begin{align*}E_n+2&=2(E_{n-1}+2)\\\\E_n+2&=2^{n-1}(E_1+2)\\\\E_n+2&=2^{n+1}\\\\E_n&=2^{n+1}-2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe expected number is $2^{n+1}-2$ tosses.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      }
    ]
  },
